The common part of both strings can stay as it is. Everything else must be removed from the first string or inserted into it. So I first find the longest common subsequence, or LCS.

If the LCS length is `common`, the answer is `str1.length() - common` deletions plus `str2.length() - common` insertions.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
public:
    int minOperations(string str1, string str2) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(str1.size());
        int m = static_cast<int>(str2.size());
        vector<vector<int>> dp(n + 1, vector<int>(m + 1, 0));

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= m; ++j) {
                if (str1[i - 1] == str2[j - 1]) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
                }
            }
        }

        int common = dp[n][m];
        return (n - common) + (m - common);
    }
};
```

For example, with `str1 = "heap"` and `str2 = "pea"`, the LCS is `"ea"` with length 2. We delete `h` and `p`, then insert the missing `p`, so the result is 3.

Time complexity: `O(n * m)`
Space complexity: `O(n * m)`

A recursive choice works nicely here. At positions `i` and `j`, matching characters are kept and both pointers move forward. If they differ, one option deletes from `str1` and the other inserts a character needed by `str2`.

The memo table prevents solving the same pair of positions again. After finding the longest common subsequence length, the remaining characters give the required operations.

```java
class Solution {
    private int[][] memo;
    private String first;
    private String second;

    public int minOperations(String str1, String str2) {
        first = str1;
        second = str2;
        memo = new int[str1.length()][str2.length()];

        for (int i = 0; i < str1.length(); i++) {
            java.util.Arrays.fill(memo[i], -1);
        }

        int common = lcs(0, 0);
        return str1.length() + str2.length() - 2 * common;
    }

    private int lcs(int i, int j) {
        if (i == first.length() || j == second.length()) {
            return 0;
        }
        if (memo[i][j] != -1) {
            return memo[i][j];
        }

        if (first.charAt(i) == second.charAt(j)) {
            memo[i][j] = 1 + lcs(i + 1, j + 1);
        } else {
            memo[i][j] = Math.max(lcs(i + 1, j), lcs(i, j + 1));
        }
        return memo[i][j];
    }
}
```

An empty string is handled by the base case. For example, converting `"abc"` to `""` needs three deletions, while converting `""` to `"xy"` needs two insertions.

Time complexity: `O(n * m)`
Space complexity: `O(n * m)` for the memo table, plus recursion stack space.

This version stores the result for each pair of indexes in a map. The recursive function returns the length of the longest common subsequence from those indexes onward.

When the current characters match, keeping that character is always useful. Otherwise, the code tries skipping one character from either string and keeps the larger result.

```go
func minOperations(str1 string, str2 string) int {
    memo := make(map[[2]int]int)

    var lcs func(int, int) int
    lcs = func(i int, j int) int {
        if i == len(str1) || j == len(str2) {
            return 0
        }

        key := [2]int{i, j}
        if value, ok := memo[key]; ok {
            return value
        }

        var best int
        if str1[i] == str2[j] {
            best = 1 + lcs(i+1, j+1)
        } else {
            skipFirst := lcs(i+1, j)
            skipSecond := lcs(i, j+1)
            if skipFirst > skipSecond {
                best = skipFirst
            } else {
                best = skipSecond
            }
        }

        memo[key] = best
        return best
    }

    common := lcs(0, 0)
    return len(str1) + len(str2) - 2*common
}
```

For `"abc"` and `"def"`, there is no shared character. The LCS length is 0, so all three characters from the first string are deleted and all three from the second are inserted. The answer is 6.

Both empty strings return 0 without entering the recursive work. The map only contains states that are actually visited.

Time complexity: `O(n * m)`
Space complexity: `O(n * m)` for cached states, with additional recursion depth up to `O(n + m)`.

Instead of storing every DP row, only the previous row and the current row are needed. For each pair of characters, a match extends the diagonal result. A mismatch takes the better result from the top or left cell.

The final LCS length tells how many characters can be shared. The rest of the characters account for the insertions and deletions.

```java
class Solution {
    public int minOperations(String str1, String str2) {
        if (str1.length() < str2.length()) {
            String temp = str1;
            str1 = str2;
            str2 = temp;
        }

        int smallLength = str2.length();
        int[] previous = new int[smallLength + 1];

        for (int i = 1; i <= str1.length(); i++) {
            int[] current = new int[smallLength + 1];

            for (int j = 1; j <= smallLength; j++) {
                if (str1.charAt(i - 1) == str2.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    current[j] = previous[j - 1] + 1;
                } else {
                    current[j] = Math.max(previous[j], current[j - 1]);
                }
            }
            previous = current;
        }

        int common = previous[smallLength];
        return str1.length() + str2.length() - 2 * common;
    }
}
```

The swap is only for memory saving, so the shorter string controls the array size. It does not affect the answer because the common subsequence is the same either way.

For `"a"` and `"b"`, the common length is zero. One deletion and one insertion are needed, giving 2 operations.

Time complexity: `O(n * m)`
Space complexity: `O(min(n, m))`
